WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.320
In chapter 1, we cut the links in a drawing, and the doublets slid apart instead of

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:08.280
bending. Now turn the question around.

3
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.880
When a real cilium stops, which part broke, and where in the body does it show?

4
00:00:14.933 --> 00:00:18.713
Summers and Gibbons tested that idea in 1971.

5
00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:22.513
They took axonemes from sea urchin sperm.

6
00:00:23.753 --> 00:00:30.053
They briefly treated them with trypsin, a protein-cutting enzyme, then added 0.1 mM

7
00:00:30.053 --> 00:00:31.053
ATP.

8
00:00:31.883 --> 00:00:35.503
Under dark-field microscopy, the axonemes did not beat.

9
00:00:36.963 --> 00:00:38.743
Groups of doublets slid out of the bundle.

10
00:00:40.023 --> 00:00:44.563
That dish test became a standard way to ask whether a flagellum's motors work.

11
00:00:45.617 --> 00:00:53.237
Now the body. In 1976, Björn Afzelius described four men whose sperm did not swim.

12
00:00:54.737 --> 00:01:00.137
Electron microscopy indicated that the dynein arms, small projections on each outer

13
00:01:00.137 --> 00:01:02.157
doublet, were missing from their cilia.

14
00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:08.370
Sperm tails and the cilia lining the airways are built on the same basic axoneme.

15
00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:13.070
So predict: if the dynein arms are missing, what else should fail?

16
00:01:18.683 --> 00:01:23.003
Three of the four men also had frequent bronchitis and sinusitis.

17
00:01:24.303 --> 00:01:29.163
Their airway cilia were not moving mucus, a sweep called mucociliary transport.

18
00:01:30.503 --> 00:01:33.423
One missing part disrupted motion in two organs.

19
00:01:34.567 --> 00:01:39.547
The match is not perfect: sperm and airway cilia differ in some dynein parts.

20
00:01:40.687 --> 00:01:43.487
And one mutation need not act the same everywhere.

21
00:01:44.847 --> 00:01:50.167
In mice lacking a protein called Cfap53, cilia on the embryonic node stopped

22
00:01:50.167 --> 00:01:55.867
completely. Windpipe and brain-ventricle cilia still beat, but abnormally.

23
00:01:56.867 --> 00:01:58.567
The instrument matters too.

24
00:02:00.227 --> 00:02:06.307
Patients with DNAH11 mutations had normal-looking nasal cilia by conventional

25
00:02:06.307 --> 00:02:07.807
electron microscopy.

26
00:02:09.107 --> 00:02:13.827
Three-dimensional electron tomography found less outer-arm volume near the base.

27
00:02:15.317 --> 00:02:17.557
Crawling cells break the same way.

28
00:02:18.917 --> 00:02:21.077
Chapter 1's clutch needed integrins to grip.

29
00:02:22.517 --> 00:02:27.497
Neutrophils without a working integrin of the beta β2 family grip the vessel wall

30
00:02:27.497 --> 00:02:33.237
poorly. They largely fail to leave the blood, so infections are poorly controlled.

31
00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:37.310
Read a phenotype backward, tissue by tissue.

32
00:02:38.710 --> 00:02:43.730
Ask which machine failed, which part is defective, which tissue you examined, and

33
00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:44.730
with what instrument.

34
00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:49.610
Motors and constraints from chapter 1 become the places to look.
